; --historical (-H) nails each element of an (amount, cost) pair to its own
; market value at the posting's date.  Before the fix, nail_down passed the
; whole market sequence with each element and failed with "Cannot convert a
; sequence to an amount".  The AAPL lot is bought at its own price, so its
; historical gain is zero.  The MSFT purchase uses a virtual cost (@), which
; records no price, so on 2024/03/01 MSFT is still worth the $60 of the
; 2024/01/01 price: 5 MSFT are worth $300 against a $250 cost, a $50 gain.
; Pairing the cost with the amount's market value would show 0 instead.

P 2024/01/01 MSFT $60

2024/01/01 Buy AAPL
    Assets:Broker    10 AAPL {$100}
    Assets:Cash     $-1000

2024/03/01 Buy MSFT
    Assets:Broker    5 MSFT (@) $50
    Assets:Cash     $-250

P 2024/02/01 AAPL $120
P 2024/04/01 MSFT $70

test bal -G -H --empty
                 $50  Assets
                 $50    Broker
                   0    Cash
--------------------
                 $50
end test

test bal --plopen -H --empty
                 $50  Assets
                 $50    Broker
                   0    Cash
--------------------
                 $50
end test

test reg -G -H --empty
24-Jan-01 Buy AAPL              Assets:Broker                     0            0
                                Assets:Cash                       0            0
24-Mar-01 Buy MSFT              Assets:Broker                   $50          $50
                                Assets:Cash                       0          $50
end test

; Without -H the gain is valued at the latest prices.
test bal -G
                $300  Assets:Broker
end test
